            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s Perseverance rover captured a first-of-its-kind view from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. The image sequence was taken on July 2, 2026, and NASA/JPL reported it on Aug. 5, 2026. In the images, Earth appears as a tiny bright dot in the Martian evening sky and is briefly occulted by Phobos — the first time humanity has captured Earth being hidden behind another object from the surface of another planet【6:1†source】.\n\nOther recent Perseverance updates:\n\n- **Ancient impact record at “Broom Point”**: NASA reported that Perseverance found evidence that a 245-foot-thick, layered rock stack on Jezero Crater’s rim was likely built by repeated asteroid impacts. The site, called the “Broom Point member,” may be more than 3.9 billion years old【6:0†source】.\n- **“Marathon” milestone**: Perseverance has driven the equivalent of a full marathon — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — across Mars, reaching that distance on its 1,890th Martian day, or sol. It did so much faster than the Opportunity rover reached the same distance【6:2†source】.\n- **Ongoing exploration beyond Jezero’s rim**: The rover is continuing work west of Jezero Crater, including imaging and studying areas such as “Arbot” and “Broom Point,” part of its broader investigation of ancient Martian environments【6:0†source】.",
